# infra/ — Terraform AWS Infrastructure

## Purpose
This directory contains all Terraform configuration for the Excelsior Deckbuilder AWS infrastructure. All resources are managed here; apply manually — CI does **not** run `terraform apply`.

## State
State is **local** (no remote backend). Coordinate with Kyle before applying to avoid conflicts. Never commit `terraform.tfstate` or `terraform.tfstate.backup`.

## File Map

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `main.tf` | Provider config (AWS, archive, random, null); no remote backend |
| `variables.tf` | All input variable declarations with defaults |
| `outputs.tf` | Exported values — CloudFront domain, S3 bucket name, EC2 info, RDS endpoints, etc. |
| `networking.tf` | VPC, subnets, internet gateway, route tables, security groups |
| `ec2.tf` | EC2 app instance (t2.micro), Elastic IP, IAM role/policies for EC2 (SSM + ECR access) |
| `rds.tf` | RDS PostgreSQL instance (db.t4g.micro) |
| `ecr.tf` | ECR Docker image repository (`op-deckbuilder-repo`) |
| `cloudfront.tf` | CloudFront CDN distribution — **apex alias** for excelsior.cards + www (Phase 0), EC2 origin via `origin.excelsior.cards`, S3 origin for card images. Min TLS `TLSv1.2_2021`; viewer_protocol_policy = `redirect-to-https`. **Default behavior** must allow `POST`/`OPTIONS`/etc. and forward cookies to EC2 or login returns **403** at the edge. |
| `s3.tf` | S3 bucket for static card image assets; OAC-only access (no public access) |
| `ssm.tf` | SSM Parameter Store — DB credentials, app env, Firebase config, CDN base URL. **`/…/app/jwt_secret`** for v1 JWT is documented in `docs/current/DEPLOYMENT.md` and is **not** a Terraform resource here—create it manually in Parameter Store when reviving infra. |
| `dns.tf` | Route 53 hosted zone + records. Phase 0: apex and www are **ALIAS** records → CloudFront; `origin.excelsior.cards` is a direct A record to the EC2 EIP for CloudFront's origin. |
| `ssl.tf` | ACM cert for `excelsior.cards` + `www` in **us-east-1** (CloudFront requirement) via the `aws.us_east_1` provider alias; DNS-validated in the main hosted zone. See [`docs/current/OPS_TLS_AND_HTTPS.md`](../docs/current/OPS_TLS_AND_HTTPS.md). |
| `ses.tf` | SES email sending config for `excelsior.cards` |
| `nginx.tf` | Nginx reverse-proxy config deployed to EC2 via SSM/user-data |
| `iam.tf` | IAM policy granting CI user S3 write access to the assets bucket |

## Key Architecture Facts

- **Current production entry point is HTTP.** HTTPS/Phase 0 files remain from an incomplete rollout; do not treat HTTPS as the working baseline unless Kyle explicitly revives it.
- **`excelsior.cards` + `www.excelsior.cards` Terraform aliases may not reflect the live HTTP baseline.** Verify current DNS before applying transport-related changes.
- **`origin.excelsior.cards` → EC2 Elastic IP directly.** This hostname is what CloudFront uses as its origin; it must NEVER be aliased to CloudFront or it creates a loop.
- **Two providers in `main.tf`**: default `us-west-2` (most resources) and `aws.us_east_1` for the ACM cert (CloudFront requirement).
- **CloudFront origins:**
  - Default behavior → EC2 via `origin.excelsior.cards` (all app traffic)
  - Ordered behavior `/src/resources/cards/images/*` → S3 bucket (card images)
  - Ordered behavior `/src/resources/images/*` → S3 bucket (UI icons and deck backgrounds)
- **CloudFront** is also still reachable at its auto-generated `*.cloudfront.net` domain. The server injects this as `CDN_BASE_URL` (SSM parameter) at container startup → `window.APP_CDN_BASE` in the frontend. After Phase 0 this is optional — the apex alias also covers card image paths.
- **S3 bucket** (`excelsior-cards-assets-dev`) is fully private; only CloudFront can read from it via OAC (Origin Access Control). Direct public HTTP access returns 403.
- **OAC not OAI** — always use `aws_cloudfront_origin_access_control` for new S3 origins. OAI is legacy and deprecated.

## CDN / Card Image Gotchas (Lessons Learned)

- **Thumbnails are not in git** — `src/resources/cards/images/characters/thumb/`, `missions/thumb/`, and `locations/thumb/` are in `.gitignore`. The `sync-images` CI job **must** run `npm run generate:thumbnails` before `aws s3 sync`, or S3 will never have thumbnails.
- **UI images ARE in git** — `src/resources/images/**/*.png` is tracked. The `sync-images` job runs `aws s3 sync src/resources/images/` so icons/backgrounds reach S3/CloudFront and can be served without EC2 image bytes.
- **Docker image has no card images** — `.dockerignore` excludes `src/resources/cards/images/`. In production, card images can only come from CloudFront/S3. Local dev uses Express static because `CDN_BASE_URL` is empty.
- **`CDN_BASE` is captured at module init time** — `public/js/deck-selection/deckTileImages.js` captures `window.APP_CDN_BASE` into a constant `CDN_BASE` when the IIFE runs. If `app-config.js` somehow hasn't executed yet, `CDN_BASE` will be `''` for the entire page load even if `window.APP_CDN_BASE` is set later. `app-config.js` must stay as a synchronous `<script>` tag before other deck-selection scripts.
- **AWS_S3_ASSETS_BUCKET secret must match Terraform output** — verify with `cd infra && terraform output -raw assets_bucket_name`. A mismatch means sync-images writes to a different bucket than CloudFront reads from.
- **Diagnosing blank images**: check `/js/app-config.js` in the browser — should return `window.APP_CDN_BASE = "https://....cloudfront.net";`. If empty, `CDN_BASE_URL` is missing from the container's `.env`.
- **Double-slash URL bug** — when building `cdnBase + '/' + path` and `path` already starts with `/`, the result is a double slash (e.g. `https://cdn.net//src/...`). Always normalize: strip trailing `/` from `cdnBase` and leading `/` from `path` before joining.

## Workflow

```bash
# Always plan before apply:
terraform plan

# Review the plan carefully — changes affect live production
terraform apply

# Get output values:
terraform output assets_bucket_name
terraform output -raw cloudfront_domain_name
```

## IAM for CI (GitHub Actions)

The CI pipeline uses `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` / `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` stored as GitHub Actions secrets. These belong to an IAM user whose username is tracked in `var.ci_iam_username`. The `iam.tf` file defines the S3 write policy and attaches it to this user.

